@page "/exgrid/editing"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Editing"
          Lead="ExGrid is display-first: nothing edits until you say so. When a column is editable, the grid runs the editor and reports what the user committed. It never writes the value itself — your application applies it, by handing back a new row.">

    <DocsSection Title="Editable columns and the Edit Intent">
        <p>
            Declare <code>editable: true</code> on a column and wire <code>OnEdit</code>. On a selected cell, the
            user types to replace the value, or presses <kbd>F2</kbd> (or double-clicks) to edit it with a caret.
            <kbd>Backspace</kbd> empties the cell and starts typing in it, as in Excel; nothing is written until the
            edit commits. <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Tab</kbd> commit and move on, cycling inside the selection, so a
            block can be filled by typing; <kbd>Escape</kbd> cancels. While typing over a value, the arrow keys,
            <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> commit and move, and <kbd>Shift</kbd> with an arrow or with
            <kbd>Home</kbd>, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> with an arrow, commit and then extend the selection or go to the
            edge from the edited cell, as they do with no edit open — as Excel's Enter mode was seen to do with
            <kbd>Shift</kbd> and an arrow, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>→</kbd>.
            After <kbd>F2</kbd> in the cell they move the caret, or select text, instead. A click on another cell
            commits too, and the click then selects as usual. A click on the cell being edited lands in the editor
            and keeps the edit, even one made at once after the first key, before the editor has appeared on a slow
            Blazor Server connection.
        </p>
        <p>
            On Blazor Server, an IME composition completed while a popover opens follows the keyboard into
            that popover. Its text is inserted at the caret of a text field, as in Find. A control that cannot
            accept composed text drops it and the keys held behind it; no Cell Editor opens behind the popover.
        </p>
        <p>
            A commit arrives as a <code>GridEditIntent&lt;TRow&gt;</code>: the row instance, the column's name and
            the text. Apply it by replacing the row with a <strong>new instance</strong> — with
            <code>GridSource.From</code>, <code>ReplaceRow(row, replacement)</code>. A row repaints when its
            instance changes; a row object rewritten in place does not repaint, and the source refuses a
            "replacement" that is the same instance. Records and <code>with</code> make this one line.
        </p>
        <p>
            The editor outlives a change of order. If the rows are reordered while the user is typing — a live
            update, a sort — the editor stays open with the text. With a Row Key it moves with its row, the Focus
            with it, and <kbd>Enter</kbd> writes to that row wherever it stands now; the grid does not scroll to
            follow it. Without a Row Key the editor stays where it is, and <kbd>Enter</kbd> is refused through
            <code>OnRefused</code> with a <code>GridRefusal.Commit</code> of <code>OrderMoved</code>, the text kept, because the position now names
            another row; <kbd>Escape</kbd> leaves without writing. An editor opened with <kbd>F2</kbd> and not typed
            into closes instead when its row leaves the Window, and, without a Row Key, when the order moves, since
            its position then names another row — once the browser has confirmed that nothing was typed into it. See
            <a href="exgrid/editing#editing-while-values-change">Editing while values change</a>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Validation">
        <p>
            A column's <code>validate</code> delegate judges each commit and answers an <code>EditVerdict</code>:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>EditVerdict.Accept</code> — apply it.</li>
            <li><code>EditVerdict.Reject(message)</code> — the editor stays open with the text, and nothing is raised. For text that cannot be read at all.</li>
            <li><code>EditVerdict.Flag(message)</code> — the edit goes through, and the cell is marked as an error. For a value that can be read but needs a second look.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A verdict judges the typed text before anything is raised. A rule that has to be checked against your
            newest data — a limit, a permission, a version — is answered after, in <code>OnEdit</code>, with
            <code>GridEditIntent.Refuse(message)</code>: see below.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>GridRuleset&lt;TRow&gt;</code> is a ready-made holder for these rules. Its
            <code>ValidatorFor</code> is what a column's <code>validate</code> is set to, and after you apply
            changes, <code>Evaluate</code> re-judges the rows so that a pasted value wears the same mark as a typed
            one. Bind its <code>State</code> and <code>Message</code> to the grid's <code>CellState</code> and
            <code>CellMessageOf</code>; rest the pointer on a flagged cell to read why.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>Column(name, rule)</code> holds a rule on one column's text, asked at the editor. A rule that spans
            the row — an end date before its start, a total that must match its parts — is added with
            <code>Row(rule)</code>, which answers a <code>RuleViolation</code> for each column that should wear the
            mark, with its message. A row rule is judged by <code>Evaluate</code>, after the change is applied, so it
            only ever Flags: at commit time the row it would judge does not exist yet, and there is nothing for it to
            refuse.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A verdict may come later.</strong> In ExGrid for React (<code>@@ex-grid/react</code>, published with
            0.3.0) a column's <code>validate</code> may answer a promise of a verdict, and <code>onEdit</code> may return a
            promise, before which <code>intent.refuse(message)</code> still holds the editor. Each is handed an
            <code>AbortSignal</code>. In Blazor's <code>ExGrid.Components.ExGrid</code> a column declares
            <code>ValidateAsync</code>, answering a <code>ValueTask&lt;EditVerdict&gt;</code> with a
            <code>CancellationToken</code>, in place of <code>Validate</code>; an asynchronous <code>OnEdit</code>,
            <code>OnPaste</code> or <code>OnFill</code> handler is waited for the same way, and its intent carries the token. Every verdict
            there, <code>Validate</code>'s too, crosses from .NET, so on Blazor Server each commit waits one round trip.
            Check the token before your handler writes: <kbd>Escape</kbd> is decided in the browser, so a cancel that
            reaches .NET after your handler wrote finds the write made, and the cell shows it under the editor. While the answer is out the commit is pending: the editor keeps the text and the
            keyboard, read-only and <code>aria-busy</code> with its outline dashed, the pointer shows the grid busy, and the
            root carries <code>data-ex-pending-commit</code> in place of the Settled Mark. Every key, press and paste made
            meanwhile waits, and is taken once the answer has come, against what it left: <code>150</code>
            <kbd>Enter</kbd> <code>200</code> <kbd>Enter</kbd> typed at once ends in the same cells as typed after each
            answer, under a Reject as under an Accept. <kbd>Escape</kbd> while it is pending cancels: the signal is
            aborted, nothing is written, the editor stays open with the text, and the grid says so. A paste whose
            <code>onPaste</code>, or a fill whose <code>onFill</code>, answers later is pending in the same way, with no
            editor, and is handed the same signal.
        </p>
        <p>
            That lets a verdict judge the object an edit would make, not only its text. Compose it from the row and the
            text, and ask your domain whether it can exist, on a server if it has to be; a Reject on a domain invariant is
            what the editor is held for. A <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> fill asks the verdict once, for the row the
            editor was opened on, so the other rows of a fill are judged in <code>onPaste</code>, where the whole target is
            in hand: apply the objects that can exist, and refuse the fill, or flag the rest.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A domain that answers later, over the Script Core" Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreDomainGrid)" Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            Type <code>1500</code> into a Notional and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>: no trade of that size can be booked,
            and the editor holds. Type <code>60000000</code>: it is booked, painted as an error, and its reason opens
            when the Focus stands on it. Press <kbd>Escape</kbd> while the answer is out to cancel the commit. Flag a
            few, sort by another column, and step through them with <kbd>F8</kbd> and <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F8</kbd>.
        </DocsExample>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const columns: GridColumn&lt;Trade&gt;[] = [{
    name: 'Notional', type: 'number', editable: true, value: trade =&gt; trade.notional,
    validate: async (trade, text, signal) =&gt; {
        const candidate = { ...trade, notional: Number(text) };      // the object the edit would make
        const problem = await domain.check(candidate, { signal });  // your domain, wherever it runs
        return problem === undefined ? { kind: 'accept' } : { kind: 'reject', message: problem };
    },
}];</code></pre>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Rules over the whole collection">
        <p>
            Some rules hold over many rows at once. A trade blotter may require one discount curve per CSA: change one
            trade's curve and its whole CSA breaks the rule, not that trade alone. Such a rule <strong>never holds the
            editor</strong>, because the way to a valid collection often passes through invalid ones. Moving a CSA to
            a new curve trade by trade breaks the rule at the first edit and restores it at the last, and a refusal at
            the editor would trap the user before the second.
        </p>
        <p>
            So the edit lands. <code>GridRuleset.Set</code> declares the rule, and <code>Evaluate</code> judges it over
            your whole collection after each apply. Every cell a <strong>Breach</strong> names wears
            <code>Error</code> with its message, on screen or far below it. A Breach names its rows by Row Key, so a
            ruleset that holds a set rule is made with the same key function your Source declares, and one made without
            it throws. Write the message in your users' words, naming the other side by what they see, such as a trade
            id, never by a row position, which a sort changes. The refusal belongs to your own save: refuse it while
            <code>Breaches</code> is not empty, as a database checks a deferred constraint at commit.
        </p>
        <p>
            A rule that one row can break and fix on its own stays a column's <code>Validate</code>, and may reject.
            <code>FindError</code> answers the next or the previous error cell in the order the grid shows, for a step
            through every error rather than only the painted ones.
        </p>
        <p>
            In ExGrid for React (<code>@@ex-grid/react</code>, published with 0.3.0) the same ruleset is
            <code>new GridRuleset(rowKey).set(rule)</code>, and the step has its keys: give the grid
            <code>findError</code>, and <kbd>F8</kbd> brings the Focus to the next error cell and
            <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F8</kbd> to the previous, wherever it stands, as Visual Studio and VS Code step
            through problems. The grid asks it with the Focus, the visible columns in their order and the row sequence
            version, and you answer with <code>ruleset.findError(request, rowsInOrder)</code>, the rows read from your
            Source in the order it shows them, now or as a promise. An answer that arrives after the order moved moves
            nothing, and the grid says so; so does a step that finds no error. A grid without <code>findError</code>
            leaves <kbd>F8</kbd> to the page, and a grid given one refuses <kbd>F8</kbd> among its declared keys.
        </p>
        <p>
            Blazor's <code>ExGrid.Components.ExGrid</code> takes the step as <code>OnFindError</code>, a
            <code>Func&lt;GridErrorStepRequest, CancellationToken, Task&lt;GridFindResult&gt;&gt;</code> answered in .NET:
            return <code>ruleset.FindError(request, rowsInOrder)</code>, the rows read from your Source in its order, which
            an in-memory Source holds whole as its <code>Window</code>. It is asked in its turn after the edits before it,
            and a step asked under an order your Source has since left is not handed to you: the grid says the rows moved.
            So <code>OnFindError</code> takes a <code>Source</code>, and is refused beside <code>Rows</code>, whose order
            the grid keeps out of your sight. The Example above steps through its flagged trades this way.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const rules = new GridRuleset&lt;Trade&gt;(trade =&gt; trade.id).set(oneCurvePerCsa);
rules.evaluate(trades, columns);   // after each apply, over the whole collection

&lt;ExGrid source={source} columns={columns}
    cellState={rules.cellState} cellMessage={rules.cellMessage}
    findError={async (request, signal) =&gt; {
        const count = source.snapshot().totalCount;   // an in-memory Source always knows it
        if (count === undefined) throw new Error('The rows are not counted yet.');
        return rules.findError(request, await source.rows({ start: 0, count }, signal));
    }} /&gt;</code></pre>
        <DocsExample Title="One discount curve per CSA" Builtin="typeof(CollectionRules)" Mud="typeof(CollectionRulesMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/CurveTrade.cs"])">
            Change <code>T-0001</code>'s curve to <code>USD-FF</code>: every trade of CSA ABC is marked, including those
            you must scroll to, and Save is refused. Select ABC's curves and type <code>USD-FF</code> with
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>, or change them one by one: the last edit clears every mark. Under
            React the Example also takes <code>findError</code>, so <kbd>F8</kbd> steps through the marked trades.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Bulk changes, Delete and Undo">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Paste and the fill keys</strong> — <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd>
                (fill down), <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd> (fill right) and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> (type once,
                fill the selection) — all arrive as one <code>OnPaste</code>, carrying a value for every target cell.
                See <a href="exgrid/clipboard">Clipboard</a> for the shape rules.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The fill handle</strong>, with <code>ShowFillHandle</code>, is the square at the corner of the
                selection: its bottom-right, or the top-right of whole columns and the bottom-left of whole rows, one
                or several, beside the header or the Row Headings, as Excel puts it (observed beside Excel: one
                column and one row in the Windows run of 2026-10-11, several in the readings of 2026-10-12, R5). It
                stays at the edge of the part in view when that first row or column is scrolled away, as Excel's
                did for a column (R5; for a row by generalisation), and a selection of every cell has none, as
                Excel showed none (R5). Drag it and the grid raises <code>OnFill</code> with the source range and
                the target beside it; what a fill means — a copy, a series — is yours.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><kbd>Delete</kbd></strong> raises <code>OnClear</code>: these cells should hold no value.
                It is not a paste of empty text, which an amount column would read as zero. A column with no blank
                of its own can simply be left alone.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A gesture that touches a column that is not editable is refused whole</strong> through
                <code>OnRefused</code>, before anything is raised, never half-applied. The grid says so in its
                live region, naming the column — see
                <a href="exgrid/clipboard#what-a-refusal-sounds-like">what a refusal sounds like</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd></strong> (or
                <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>) are forwarded as <code>OnUndo</code> and <code>OnRedo</code>. The history is yours, because only your application sees
                every change, including those made outside the grid. Each gesture arrives as one intent, so keeping
                one step per intent makes a paste of a thousand cells one <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>. While the
                editor is open, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undoes the typing instead.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            In ExGrid for React the same are <code>showFillHandle</code>, <code>onPaste</code>, <code>onFill</code>,
            <code>onClear</code>, <code>onUndo</code> and <code>onRedo</code>, and a refused gesture reaches
            <code>onRefused</code> as <code>{ kind: 'paste', reason }</code>. A column's <code>validate</code> answers
            <code>{ kind: 'accept' }</code>, or <code>'reject'</code> or <code>'flag'</code> with a message.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="An editable blotter with validation and undo" Builtin="typeof(EditingGrid)" Mud="typeof(EditingGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])">
            Type <code>200000</code> into a quantity to see a flag, or <code>abc</code> to see a rejection. Select a
            few cells and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd>, drag the fill handle, press <kbd>Delete</kbd> on
            traders, then undo each step. A paste that holds text a quantity or a price cannot hold is refused whole
            with <code>Refuse()</code>, and the Example names the text, rather than writing the cells around it.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Editing while values change">
        <p>
            Under live data a value can change between the moment the user sees it and the moment their write
            lands. ExGrid's rule is that <strong>a write lands as the user entered it, on the row they aimed it
            at</strong>. If a cell showed 100, the feed moved it to 110, and the user commits 125, <code>OnEdit</code>
            receives 125 with the row as it is now. The same holds for a paste, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>,
            <kbd>Delete</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>, the fill handle and an
            Action press: no write is refused because a value changed. The cells were on screen, and a live change
            there wears the Change Highlight.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>The one change the user cannot see is the one under the open editor</strong>, which covers the
            cell. When a commit lands over a cell whose text changed while the editor was open, the grid raises
            <code>OnOverwriteNotice</code> with the cell, the text the user saw and the text the commit replaced.
            The grid holds no sentence for it: your Chrome or your page words it, into a live region or a toast,
            and may offer to put the replaced value back. The user's own earlier writes count as seen — typing
            <code>5</code> <kbd>Enter</kbd> <kbd>↑</kbd> <code>7</code> <kbd>Enter</kbd> never reports the 5 as a
            change, however fast it is typed and whether the 5 comes back before the <code>7</code> or under it.
            A write of the user's settles with the first new Window the grid takes in after your handler for it
            has completed; with a bound source, the grid asks the source to put out what it gathered at that
            moment, so a write your handler handed it is in the Window before the next key is handled. What the
            user saw is only ever read from the edited row: should that row be out of the Window as the write
            settles, the editor keeps what it opened over, and the commit may tell the user's own value as a
            change — it never hides one.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>The Edit Intent carries the same two texts</strong>, <code>SeenText</code> and
            <code>ReplacedText</code>; the notice is raised exactly when they differ. A Consumer that must not write
            over a change — or whose store, a reducer or a server, is where writes are ordered — calls
            <code>Refuse(message)</code> before its handler completes: the editor stays open with the typing, the
            message is shown at the editor and announced, and the gesture moves nothing. A later commit raises the
            intent again. Your handler may take its time — a round trip to a server — and the editor stands while
            it runs. A press made meanwhile, the second press of a double click or a click on a header included,
            waits for your answer and is then answered against what it left, exactly as if you had answered at
            once: one commit raises one Edit Intent.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Target identity still matters</strong>, so a write is refused when the grid can no longer find
            what it was aimed at, and the callback names which of these happened. A paste, a fill or
            <kbd>Delete</kbd> refused as <code>OrderMoved</code> or <code>SourceChanged</code> is also said by the
            grid, in its live region and in the built-in Chrome's words, as every refused write is
            (<a href="exgrid/clipboard#what-a-refusal-sounds-like">what a refusal sounds like</a>). For the rest —
            a commit or an Action refused through <code>OnRefused</code>, and <code>OnEditDiscarded</code> — the grid
            holds no string: the words are yours.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>OrderMoved</code> — the Row Sequence Version moved after the gesture was aimed: the Selection a
                paste, a fill or <kbd>Delete</kbd> was aimed with no longer names those rows
                (a <code>GridRefusal.Paste</code>); without a Row Key, an editor's position names another row
                (a <code>GridRefusal.Commit</code>); a press on an Action that only a position names
                (a <code>GridRefusal.Action</code>). Typing — a character, <kbd>F2</kbd>, <kbd>Backspace</kbd>, an IME's
                text — that reaches the grid after the order moved, typed against the Selection the move dropped,
                opens no editor and writes nothing anywhere, rather than landing on the first cell of the new order:
                <code>OnEditDiscarded</code> says <code>OrderMoved</code>, once for the run of keys. A key typed once
                the new order is on screen opens an edit as the first key always does.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>RowLeftTheWindow</code> — the row is not in the rows the grid holds now: with a Row Key, no row
                in the Window answers the key of the edited or pressed row. It may have left the data or only been
                scrolled out of a pushed or fetched Window; bring it back into view and commit or press again, or
                press <kbd>Escape</kbd>. Without a Row Key, an open edit whose row left the Window goes, and
                <code>OnEditDiscarded</code> says <code>RowLeftTheWindow</code>. An editor that holds nothing typed —
                opened with <kbd>F2</kbd>, a double-click or a press into the Formula Bar, its text still the cell's
                value — is not kept waiting for its row: it holds a copy of your data, which the grid does not keep,
                so when a Window without its row comes in, it closes as <kbd>Escape</kbd> would close it. Without a Row
                Key its row is the one at its position under the order it opened under, so a Window under another order
                closes it too. It closes only once the browser has confirmed that its field still holds the cell's value
                and nothing is being composed there: on Blazor Server the grid's own copy of the text is a round trip
                behind the typing, so a character typed as the Window moves keeps the edit open with it, as any typing
                does. The keys and presses that follow the browser's answer wait until the editor has closed, and are
                then the grid's. Nothing is written or discarded, and <code>OnEditingChanged</code> says the edit
                ended.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>SourceChanged</code> — the <code>Source</code> was replaced by another instance. That is a new
                binding whatever the two sources' Row Sequence Versions are: the Selection is dropped, an open edit is
                discarded and <code>OnEditDiscarded</code> says <code>SourceChanged</code> — as it does for typing
                aimed at what the old source painted, with a cell selected or none, which opens nothing — and a
                paste, a fill,
                <kbd>Delete</kbd> or an Action aimed at what the old source painted is refused rather than landing
                on the new source's row at the same place. A <code>Source</code> your own <code>OnEdit</code> handler
                hands over is that commit's doing, and so are other visible columns it shows: the edit ends as
                committed and nothing is said discarded, and only an intent you refused is discarded, as
                <code>SourceChanged</code> or <code>ColumnsChanged</code>. No Overwrite Notice follows such a commit;
                the intent you handled carried both texts.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            An accepted Action reaches <code>OnAction</code> once, with the row as it is now.
            While it waits behind earlier input, the row must remain in the Window: leaving it
            invalidates the waiting command even if the row returns, with <code>RowLeftTheWindow</code>
            reported through <code>OnRefused</code>. Scrolling it outside the painted slice is harmless. Whether the command is still
            allowed — a price limit, a status — is your business rule there, and your page says why it declines.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A feed, a user's write and the Consumer's decisions" Builtin="typeof(LiveWrites)" Mud="typeof(LiveWritesMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])">
            Select a Price and type <code>125</code>, press <kbd>F9</kbd> to advance the feed while the editor stays
            open, then <kbd>Enter</kbd>: 125 is written, and the page says what it replaced. Type <code>175</code>:
            this Consumer refuses prices above 150 with <code>Refuse(message)</code>, and the editor keeps your
            typing; <kbd>Escape</kbd> leaves it. Press <kbd>F9</kbd> until a price passes 150 and press its
            Approve: the grid delivers the Action, and this page declines it, saying why. In React the refusal is
            <code>edit.refuse(message)</code>, the notice <code>onOverwriteNotice</code>, and <kbd>F9</kbd> is declared
            with <code>declaredKeys</code> and heard by <code>onDeclaredKey</code>; the feed is a Change Batch handed to
            the keyed source's <code>apply</code>.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

